          "type": "text",
          "text": "Submitted. Summary of findings from the synchronous `Retrying` control flow in `tenacity/__init__.py`:\n\n1. **after on accepted success — false.** `_post_retry_check_actions` returns the outcome immediately (`rs.outcome.result()`) when the retry predicate rejects the result; `self.after` is only enqueued on the retry path.\n2. **wait computed before stop — true.** `_run_wait` is enqueued before `_run_stop` in `_post_retry_check_actions`, and `iter` executes actions in list order, so `upcoming_sleep` is set before `stop_run_result` is evaluated.\n3. **before_sleep on the stopping attempt — false.** When `stop_run_result` is true, `_post_stop_check_actions` returns after enqueueing the error callback/`exc_check`; the `before_sleep` action is only added in the not-stopped branch alongside `DoSleep`.\n4. **retry_error_callback replaces exhaustion error — true.** On stop, if `retry_error_callback` is set it is enqueued instead of `exc_check` (which raises `RetryError`/reraises), and its return value flows through `iter` as `do`, which `Retrying.__call__` returns.",
